<section
  id='case-studiobid'
  class='fullscreen-section case-study-section'
  data-title='Case Study: StudioBid — Web Development | Katja Turnsek'
  data-description='Vanilla JavaScript auction platform case study focused on API integration, authentication, bidding, credits, listings, profiles, and reusable UI states.'
  data-case='studiobid'
  aria-labelledby='case-studiobid-title'
>
  <div class='rows'>
    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-header'>
        <p class='case-eyebrow'>Web Development</p>
        <h1 id='case-studiobid-title'>StudioBid</h1>
        <p class='case-subtitle'>An API-driven auction platform for browsing, selling, and bidding
          on creative work.</p>

        <dl class='case-summary' aria-label='Project summary'>
          <div>
            <dt>Role</dt>
            <dd>UX/UI design and front-end development</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Scope</dt>
            <dd>Individual Semester Project 2</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Year</dt>
            <dd>2025</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Tools</dt>
            <dd>JavaScript, Bootstrap, Sass, Noroff API</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Outcome</dt>
            <dd>Complete auction journey</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>

        <div class='tech-demo-wrapper case-demo-wrapper'>
          <div class='demo-links' data-auto='demo' data-case='studiobid'></div>
        </div>

        <nav
          class='project-switcher'
          aria-label='StudioBid sections'
          data-auto='switcher'
          data-case='studiobid'
        ></nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>The brief</h2>
      <p>
        The goal was to turn the Noroff Auction API into a coherent product where users could
        discover listings, place bids, create auctions, manage their profiles, and understand their
        available credits. The project needed to connect these actions across several views while
        keeping the experience predictable for both visitors and authenticated users.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-grid'>
        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--challenge'>
          <h2>Challenge</h2>
          <p>
            Auction data changes constantly. Bid values, credit balances, listing status, and time
            remaining all affect which actions should be available. The main challenge was keeping
            those connected states understandable across browsing, listing details, forms, and user
            profiles.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Protect account actions while keeping public listings easy to explore</li>
            <li>Reflect bids and credit changes clearly after an API action</li>
            <li>Handle loading, validation, empty results, and API errors without breaking the
              journey</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--browse'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/site-studiobid-600.webp'
            data-src='site-studiobid-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='400'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='StudioBid homepage showing search, sorting, auction cards, current bids, and time remaining'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>Searchable auction listings with bid status and time
            remaining</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--decisions'>
          <h2>Key decisions</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Separated API, authentication, rendering, and form behavior into reusable JavaScript
              modules so related features could share the same logic
            </li>
            <li>
              Used persistent authentication and protected actions to keep browsing public while
              reserving bidding and listing management for signed-in users
            </li>
            <li>
              Designed listing cards and detail views around the information needed for a bidding
              decision: price, time remaining, seller, media, and bid history
            </li>
            <li>
              Created reusable validation and feedback helpers so forms respond consistently to
              success, invalid input, and API errors
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--validation'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/site-studiobid-code-600.webp'
            data-src='site-studiobid-code-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='402'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Code editor showing a reusable StudioBid form validation helper for valid and invalid field states'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>Reusable form validation and feedback logic</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row case-outcome'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>Outcome and reflection</h2>
      <p>
        The completed app connects listing discovery, authentication, bidding, credit visibility,
        profile management, and listing creation in one responsive experience. StudioBid
        strengthened how I structure larger vanilla JavaScript projects and showed me how much clear
        feedback and predictable state handling matter when several parts of a product depend on the
        same changing API data.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='back-to-work-container'>
      <a
        href='{{base}}#work'
        class='label-link back-to-work-label'
        data-back
        data-back-fallback='{{base}}#work'
        aria-label='Back to Work'
      >
        <svg class='icon icon-18 arrow-icon' aria-hidden='true'><use href='#i-arrow-left' /></svg>
        <span class='label-text'>Back to Work</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>